Scores

1. Use of Switch: 9/10. Switch carries the whole design: one template sets up the agents, the exclusive editor role, per-agent addressing, room documents and permission cards. Without Switch the project falls back to a script, and the README says so.
2. Technical execution: 8/10. Setup is reproducible step by step, there are 240+ offline tests, and the run log is honest and checked against git. One serious bug was found, fixed and pinned with tests. It runs only on macOS, and the full setup is heavy.
3. Creativity and impact: 7/10. "The model proposes, code decides" answers real distrust of AI agents that say "all done". But the main use case is contest submissions, which is narrow. Release-readiness gating is the bigger story, and the README only hints at it.
4. Presentation and documentation: 6/10. The README is clear and well organised, with a diagram and a section on what was reused versus built. But the demo video is missing ("coming soon"), and the video is half of this criterion.

Shortlist: Yes. It uses Switch deeply, it shows its evidence, and its engineering is honest, which puts it near the top if the video arrives.

Weaknesses that cost the most

1. No demo video. Judges cannot see the permission cards, the Decline and Allow-once flow, or the fresh-agent test. These are the best scenes in the project, and right now they exist only in text.
2. The problem framing is narrow. Checking a hackathon submission feels like a tool made for this contest. The README does not lead with the general case: a release gate or merge-readiness gate for real teams. The GitHub Action is buried in a FAQ.
3. Setup is heavy and the agent mix is thin. It needs macOS, Switch Console 0.40.x, Discord bot setup with nine permissions, and a local kit install. All five agents ran on Claude Code, so the "independent second provider" Reviewer was never shown. The approval hook only reads command text, so code inside a script or `python3 -c` gets past it.

TOTAL: 7.7
